function sacred(){
  return '<div class="imd-sacred"><b>A sacred site, in the study’s own words</b>' +
    '<p>“This stretch of the Grand River is home to the Grand River burial mounds, an ancient cemetery for Indigenous ancestors. The area remains a sacred and protected space, visited by Anishinabek for meditation, prayer, and spiritual practices. Any decisions on the Mounds site and adjacent Indian Mounds Drive are guided by Native voices and Tribal consultation.” (' + pp(24) + ') The site is a National Historic Landmark (' + pp(4) + '). “Ownership and management of the Grand River Burial Mounds site is unchanged, and will continue to be stewarded by local tribes and the Grand Rapids Public Museum.” (' + pp(54) + ')</p>' +
    '<p style="margin:0">The Kent County survey summary puts it plainly: any decisions about visibility, access, interpretation or educational materials about the mounds must be tribe-led. River Brain follows that. Nothing on this page or in the 3D model locates, draws or interprets the mounds beyond what the study itself publishes.</p></div>';
}
function intro(){
  var body = '<div class="imd-cols">' +
    '<div><p>For thousands of years, the Grand River and the surrounding landscape have been home to the Anishinaabe people, whose enduring relationship with these lands and waters continues today. The report acknowledges the Anishinaabe community and recognizes their deep cultural, historical, and spiritual connection to this region. The presence of the Grand River Burial Mounds site, a National Historic Landmark within the study area, further reflects the enduring significance of this landscape.</p>' +
    '<p>Few places in West Michigan match the ecological richness, cultural significance, and recreational value of Indian Mounds Drive: a critical 4-mile link in the emerging Grand River Greenway where several trail systems and recreational destinations converge. From 2025 to 2026, thousands of community members participated in this study. The resulting plan revitalizes Indian Mounds Drive as a recreational destination that is connective, thoughtfully managed, and inviting.</p></div>' +
    '<div><h4 class="imd-h4">Public input process</h4>' +
    '<p class="imd-h5">Phase 1: Site Exploration & Data Collection</p>' + list(['Focus groups with governments, local Tribal governments, partners, stakeholders, and user groups','On-site yard signs','Online community survey','Media outreach']) +
    '<p class="imd-h5">Phase 2: Scenario Development</p>' + list(['Repeated focus groups, reacting to design scenarios','On-site yard signs','Postcards','Media outreach','Community open house']) +
    '<p class="imd-h5">Phase 3: Final Recommendations & Report</p>' + list(['Community plan reveal open house']) + '</div>' +
    '<div><h4 class="imd-h4">How to use this report</h4><p>The report tells the story of Indian Mounds Drive in past, present, and future.</p>' +
    '<p><b>Part 01, Site Exploration.</b> Site discovery: history, existing conditions, inventory, usership, and public perceptions.</p><p><b>Part 02, Recommendations & Implementation.</b> Guiding principles, recommendations, and implementation strategy.</p><p><b>Appendix.</b> Supplemental analysis, outreach documentation, and data.</p>' +
    '<h4 class="imd-h4">Timeline, from the county page</h4><p>Phase 1, Fall 2025: research and input. Phase 2, Spring 2026: scenarios, refined with a second round of input. Phase 3, Summer 2026: preferred scenario and implementation plan. Study finished Fall 2026. The study was funded by Downtown Grand Rapids Inc. via a 2022 Michigan DNR grant to grow the Grand River Greenway.</p></div></div>';
  return page('intro', 'Introduction<em>Project purpose and approach</em>', '', body, 0, '4-5');
}
function context(){
  var body = '<div class="imd-cols two"><div><p>The Indian Mounds Drive corridor offers a striking contrast between a developed landscape and the natural environment. Located within the city boundaries of Grand Rapids, Wyoming, and Grandville, the project area sits between the industrial lands to the southeast, separated by the elevated Interstate 196 corridor, and the expansive natural and recreational assets of Millennium Park to the northwest, across the Grand River.</p><p>Trail connections in every direction, including two river crossings, the Wilson Avenue Bridge and the Kent Trails Bridge to Millennium Park, position Indian Mounds Drive as a vital connection to communities, regional trails, parks, and recreation opportunities, all less than three miles from downtown Grand Rapids.</p>' +
    '<p>The focus area encompasses four miles of Indian Mounds Drive and 363 acres of surrounding landscape. The former two-way road accommodates one-way vehicular traffic northbound and a shared-use trail on the other lane (' + pp(8) + ').</p></div>' +
    '<div>' + legend([['dash','#555','City boundary',''],['line','#ec1090','Kent Trails',''],['line','#5488b0','CSX rail line',''],['line','#f2c318','Indian Mounds Drive',''],['head','Land use'],['blob','#e8463c','Commercial',''],['blob','#b48ad6','Industry',''],['blob','#9fd3e6','Offices / services',''],['blob','#e05aa0','Planned unit development',''],['blob','#2f64c8','Central business district',''],['blob','#c9a0e0','Residential',''],['blob','#f2e26a','Suburban residential',''],['blob','#8fd36b','Agriculture','']]) + '</div></div>' +
    '<p class="imd-note" style="margin-top:12px">The study’s context map is an oblique aerial looking north-east over Grandville, with the corridor drawn as a yellow line between I-196 and the river. The 3D model above reproduces that view on real geography; the land-use colours above are the map’s legend.</p>';
  return page('context', 'Project context map', 'Grandville, Wyoming and Grand Rapids share the corridor; Walker and Millennium Park face it across the river.', body, 0, '6-7');
}
function part(n, title, text, id){
  return '<div class="imd-part" id="imd-' + id + '"><b>Part 0' + n + '</b><span>' + title + '</span><p>' + text + '</p></div>';
}
function history(){
  var body = '<div class="imd-timeline">' + D.history.map(function(e){
    return '<div class="ev"><div class="era">' + esc(e.era) + '</div><div class="ph">' + (e.t === 'burial mounds' ? '' : esc(e.t)) + '</div><div class="yr">' + esc(e.yr) + '</div><div class="tk"></div><h5>' + esc(e.t) + '</h5><p>' + esc(e.s) + '</p></div>';
  }).join('') + '</div><p class="imd-note">The study pairs each event with an archival photograph (GRPL, GRHC, Grand Lady, Roy Luck, Joshua G. Cohen). The photographs are not reproduced here; the text is the study’s. Scroll sideways.</p>';
  return page('history', 'Site history', 'The story of the land that is now Indian Mounds Drive continues from thousands of years ago. After thousands of years as an Indigenous homeland and cemetery, the 19th and 20th centuries brought land transfers, industry, waste disposal, and environmental challenges. The site has maintained its natural and cultural value and remains an important escape into nature for the community.', body, 1, '10-11');
}
function inventory(){
  var body = '<div class="imd-cols two"><div>' + legend(D.inventory) + '</div>' +
    '<div><p>The Indian Mounds Drive (IMD) corridor is primarily characterized by the river, the road, and the landscape. The one-way road begins at the south end near Wilson Ave. and ends at Market Ave. Cars can only legally enter IMD from Wilson Ave.</p>' +
    '<p>These same layers drive the 3D model: open water, wetland, the floodway and 100-year floodplain (from the FEMA National Flood Hazard Layer, which is what the study’s dashed and solid lines represent), trailheads, the recreational trail network and the access points. The study’s hatched “areas of disturbance” are not mapped in the model because their outlines exist only in the PDF.</p>' +
    '<a class="imd-btn" href="#imd-model" data-view="inventory">' + icon('cube') + ' See the inventory in 3D</a></div></div>';
  return page('inventory', 'Site inventory map', 'The Indian Mounds Drive (IMD) corridor is primarily characterized by the river, the road, and the landscape.', body, 1, '12-13');
}
function ownership(){
  var body = '<div class="imd-cols two"><div><h4 class="imd-h4">Title search findings</h4><p>The existing Indian Mounds Drive roadway crosses three river edge parcels owned by the City of Grandville, City of Grand Rapids and City of Wyoming from south to north. The City of Grand Rapids parcel is located within the City of Wyoming corporate limits, and a Wyoming-owned parcel is located in the City of Grandville. It is unclear from the title search if a defined right-of-way exists for the roadway.</p><p>Adjacent properties to the east of IMD are owned by Kent County, Goodale Enterprises (private), City of Wyoming, City of Grand Rapids and Outdoor Systems (private). Access rights from Indian Mounds Drive to these parcels exist.</p></div>' +
    '<div>' + legend(D.ownership.map(function(o){ return ['blob', o[0], o[1], o[2]]; })) + '<p class="imd-note" style="margin-top:10px">Parcel outlines are drawn only in the study’s map, so the 3D model does not colour ownership. Open the PDF page for the map.</p></div></div>';
  return page('ownership', 'Land ownership', 'Both private and public entities own property and maintain access rights to their respective parcels.', body, 1, '14-15');
}
function traffic(){
  var t = D.traffic;
  var sites = '<table><tr><th>Site</th><th></th><th class="num">Pedestrians</th><th class="num">Cyclists</th><th class="num">Motorists</th></tr>' + t.sites.map(function(s){
    return '<tr><td><b>' + s[0] + '</b></td><td>' + esc(s[1]) + '</td><td class="num" style="color:#9cac94">' + s[2].toLocaleString() + '</td><td class="num" style="color:#5488b0">' + s[3].toLocaleString() + '</td><td class="num" style="color:#e05430">' + (s[4] === null ? '-'.replace('-','n/a') : s[4].toLocaleString()) + '</td></tr>';
  }).join('') + '</table>';
  var body = '<div class="imd-cols two"><div><p>Over a seven-day period from ' + t.period + ', five sites along Indian Mounds Drive captured three transit modes: driving, cycling, and walking/running. The findings reveal higher proportions of non-motorized users (60% cyclists + pedestrians) when compared to vehicular traffic (40%). Hotspots for non-motorized uses are at Site #4 (Kent Trails Bridge to Millennium Park) and Sites #1 and #2 near Grandville Waterfront Park. Site #5 at the Market Ave. connection is not frequented by cyclists and pedestrians.</p>' +
    '<p>Although vehicular traffic has only one designated entry (at the south end) and exit (onto Market Ave. at the north), the study found an average of <b>8% wrong-way vehicles</b> over the seven day period, with nearly twice as many wrong-way vehicles observed at the beginning of the one-way road at the south, suggesting many vehicles enter from Wilson Ave, illegally turn around, and exit.</p>' +
    '<p>Activity was highest during daytime hours, although vehicular traffic was observed at all times of day with peaks at lunchtime and evening. Cycling was highest at mid-day and walking/running spiked around 8am and 7pm. Across all modes, activity was greatest on Friday, Saturday, and Sunday, with Wednesday and Tuesday the lowest.</p>' +
    donut(t.split) + '</div>' +
    '<div><h4 class="imd-h4">Activity map, seven-day counts</h4>' + sites + '<img width="717" height="375" src="' + ART + 'chart-time.webp" alt="Activity by time of day at Site 2: pedestrians, cyclists and motorists" style="width:100%;max-width:520px;display:block;margin:8px 0 4px" loading="lazy"><p class="imd-note">Activity by time of day at Site #2, from the study (p. 16). <a href="#imd-model" data-view="traffic">Show the five count sites in 3D</a>.</p></div></div>';
  return page('traffic', 'Traffic study', '', body, 1, '16-17');
}
function recreation(){
  var body = '<div class="imd-cols">' +
    '<div>' + legend([['blob','#7fae8d','Grand River Greenway','A growing network of parks, trails, and natural areas connecting communities along the Grand River, from Lowell to Lake Michigan'],['line','#f0a830','Indian Mounds Drive trail',''],['img','leg-ped.png','Pedestrian access point',''],['img','leg-boat.png','Boat launch',''],['img','leg-kayak.png','Kayak launch',''],['img','leg-trailhead.png','Trailhead',''],['line','#5f8f7e','Recreational trail networks','']]) + '</div>' +
    '<div><h4 class="imd-h4">Johnson Park</h4>' + list(D.johnson) + '<h4 class="imd-h4">Millennium Park</h4>' + list(D.millennium) + '</div>' +
    '<div><h4 class="imd-h4">Future project: Wilson Ave. Bridge</h4><p>This key river crossing connects Grandville and Walker near the south end of Indian Mounds Drive. The bridge currently carries four traffic lanes and a 5-foot sidewalk separated by a concrete barrier. The community identifies this connection as a pain point, with insufficient width for comfortable walking and biking. Future improvements should prioritize non-motorized comfort and safety.</p>' +
    '<h4 class="imd-h4">Future project: Market Ave.</h4><p>Today, Market Avenue has bike lanes on both sides beginning at the Indian Mounds Drive/I-196 on-ramp intersection. Northbound cyclists from Indian Mounds Drive must cross three lanes, including two freeway ramps. Future studies should explore intersection improvements and/or replace the bike lanes with a separated shared-use path on the northwest side of the street.</p>' +
    '<p class="imd-note">Connections named on the map: Kent Trails Bridge to Millennium Park, Kent Trails North Trailhead, Kent Trails Ivanrest Trailhead, Kent Trails freeway underpass, Butterworth Trail connection, Buck Creek Trail, to Idema Explorers Trail, Ottawa County and Grand Haven, to Oxford Trail, Grand River Edges and downtown Grand Rapids, to Fred Meijer Standale Trail, Kent Trails to Fred Meijer M-6 Trail and Paul Henry Thornapple Trail, and the Grand River Water Trail.</p></div></div>';
  return page('recreation', 'Recreational context', 'The opportunity to revitalize Indian Mounds Drive extends well beyond the four-mile corridor itself; it is a critical link within the Grand River Greenway and nearby trail networks and recreational amenities, including Millennium Park.', body, 1, '18-19');
}
function regional(){
  var body = '<div class="imd-cols two"><div><h4 class="imd-h4">Improving a critical link in the Grand River Greenway</h4><p>Indian Mounds Drive occupies a strategically important position within the emerging Grand River Greenway, a regional initiative creating a continuous system of parks, trails, natural areas, and river access points along the Grand River. Connecting communities from Lowell to Lake Michigan along <b>more than 80 miles of trail</b>, the primary connection is aimed to be completed by <b>2031</b>.</p><p>Today, Indian Mounds Drive provides connections between the Grand River Greenway, Kent Trails, Millennium Park, and the communities of Grand Rapids, Wyoming, Grandville, and Walker. However, the corridor currently functions as an aging and deteriorating segment within this network despite its high visibility and unique natural and cultural resources.</p><p>The corridor traverses floodplain forests, wetlands, and river shoreline habitats. Community engagement identified strong public interest in safer non-motorized facilities, stronger connections to the Grand River, environmental restoration, and greater interpretation of the area’s natural history.</p></div>' +
    '<div><img width="970" height="792" src="' + ART + 'map-regional.webp" alt="Regional map of the Grand River Greenway from Lake Michigan to Lowell with Indian Mounds Drive marked" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--i-rule);border-radius:4px" loading="lazy"><p class="imd-note">The study’s regional map (p. 21): the Grand River Greenway trail from Grand Haven through Greater Grand Rapids to Lowell, with Indian Mounds Drive marked. Photographs on the page: Grand Ravines Park (Georgetown Township), Lowell Timber Footbridge, the Grand River Water Trail.</p></div></div>';
  return page('regional', 'Regional connectivity', '', body, 1, '20-21');
}
function input(){
  var body = '<div class="imd-cols two"><div>' +
    '<h4 class="imd-h4">Public input process</h4><div class="imd-process"><div><h5>Phase 1: Exploration & Data Collection</h5><em>August 2025 to December 2025</em><p>The project team conducted a public online survey as well as focus group meetings with user groups, stakeholders, governments, and representatives of local Indigenous Tribes to understand issues faced today and priorities for the future.</p></div><div class="ar">→</div><div><h5>Phase 2: Scenario Development</h5><em>January 2026 to May 2026</em><p>Following design scenario development, feedback was gathered from a community open house and focus groups, coming together to determine a preferred plan. The study findings were revealed at a plan reveal open house.</p></div></div>' +
    '<h4 class="imd-h4">Focus group participants</h4><div class="imd-box"><div class="imd-cols"><div><h5>' + icon('people') + ' User groups</h5><ul><li>Anglers + water recreation</li><li>Cyclists</li><li>Event planners (Grand Rapids Marathon & River Bank Run 25k)</li></ul><h5>' + icon('plant') + ' Stakeholders</h5><ul><li>Grand Rapids Public Museum</li><li>Grand Valley Metro Council</li><li>Kent Conservation District</li><li>Lower Grand River Organization of Watersheds</li><li>Private property owners</li></ul></div><div><h5>' + icon('building') + ' Governments</h5><ul><li>City of Grand Rapids</li><li>City of Grandville</li><li>City of Wyoming</li><li>Gun Lake Tribe</li><li>Grand River Bands of Ottawa Indians</li><li>Kent County Parks Department</li><li>Kent County Road Commission</li><li>Michigan Department of Natural Resources</li><li>Michigan Department of Transportation</li></ul></div></div></div></div>' +
    '<div><div class="imd-stat badge"><span class="imd-badge lg">1,984</span><span>Public online survey responses</span></div><div class="imd-stat badge"><span class="imd-badge">107</span><span>Participants at Open House #1<small>Community members attended the event to share ideas, concerns, and hopes for the future. The county page dates it April 27, 2026, at the Kent County Parks Department.</small></span></div>' +
    '<div class="imd-stat"><b class="orange">98<sup>%</sup></b><span>of survey respondents are aware of the Indian Mounds Drive project area</span></div>' +
    '<div class="imd-cols"><div class="imd-chart"><div class="ct">Survey respondent visitation frequency</div>' + donut(D.visitation, true) + '</div><div class="imd-chart"><div class="ct">Top uses + activities</div>' + bars(D.uses) + '</div></div>' +
    '<p>A broad cross-section of the community was engaged through focus group sessions, an online survey, and open houses, gathering feedback from <b>over 2,100 participants</b>. The first phase collected user data and public perceptions and catalogued community values and challenges. The second phase, focused around feedback from design scenarios, identified common goals and guiding principles. The final plan was revealed at a celebratory open house.</p>' +
    '<h4 class="imd-h4">The 2025 survey, five themes (county page)</h4>' + list(['<b>Safety first:</b> clearly separated spaces for walking, running and biking that reduce conflicts with vehicles.','<b>Maintenance needs attention:</b> trash and dumping, invasive species, erosion, overgrown or neglected vegetation.','<b>Ecological restoration is a top priority,</b> on land and in water.','<b>Respect for sacred sites:</b> the Grand River Burial Mounds are sacred; any decisions about visibility, access, interpretation or educational materials about the mounds must be tribe-led.','<b>A calm, natural experience:</b> peaceful, natural, predictable; a place to walk, bike, fish and enjoy nature.']) + '</div></div>';
  return page('input', 'Community input', '', body, 1, '22-23');
}
function values(){
  var cards = [
    ['Urban forest','The trees are large and keep it shaded and cool for recreation.','Indian Mounds Drive feels like an escape to a wilderness in the city, with a mature tree canopy and rich flora and fauna. As a floodplain forest, the area is a refuge for some species of concern, such as Eastern Box Turtle, Rock Cress, Virginia Bluebells, and Wahoo.', '<div class="imd-stat"><b>87<sup>%</sup></b><span>desire the area to maintain its natural, quiet, wilderness feel</span></div>'],
    ['Recreation','I have decades of good memories passing through here with my family and friends.','The area is valued for its diverse recreational opportunities including biking, running, walking, fishing, wildlife viewing, and scenic drives. The site also hosts two major annual events, the River Bank Run 25K and the Grand Rapids Marathon.', '<div class="imd-chart"><div class="ct">Most important connections to Indian Mounds Drive</div>' + bars(D.connections, 'h') + '<p class="imd-note">Percent of responses, read from the study’s bar chart to the nearest few points. Other: Jenison + Ottawa County, Johnson Park + Georgetown, Wilson Ave, Grand River water access.</p></div>'],
    ['Cultural significance','Protect the historic and spiritual grounds of the Indian Mounds.','This stretch of the Grand River is home to the Grand River burial mounds, an ancient cemetery for Indigenous ancestors. The area remains a sacred and protected space, visited by Anishinabek for meditation, prayer, and spiritual practices. Any decisions on the Mounds site and adjacent Indian Mounds Drive are guided by Native voices and Tribal consultation.', ''],
    ['River access','It is nice to have easy river access for those who want to fish.','Formal access to the Grand River is mostly located at Grandville Waterfront Park, where a boat ramp and barrier-free kayak launch can be found. From Indian Mounds Drive, visitors enjoy scenic views of the Grand River and its riparian wetlands.', '<div class="imd-chart"><div class="ct">Importance of connecting to the river for recreation</div>' + donut(D.importance, true) + '</div>'],
    ['Greenway connections','Most of the time we all really enjoy the trail. It’s so convenient to get downtown on bikes.','Indian Mounds Drive, forming a link in the Grand River Greenway, is a key connection between regional trail systems. From the site, users access downtown Grand Rapids, Ottawa County trails, the Kent Trails network, and Millennium Park recreational trails.', '<div class="imd-stat"><b>+70<sup>%</sup></b><span>support conversion of Indian Mounds Drive to a non-motorized greenway trail</span></div>']
  ];
  var body = '<div class="imd-cards">' + cards.map(function(c){ return '<div class="imd-card"><div class="band"><h4>' + c[0] + '</h4></div><div class="bd"><q>' + esc(c[1]) + '</q><p>' + esc(c[2]) + '</p>' + c[3] + '</div></div>'; }).join('') + '</div>';
  return page('values', 'Community values', 'Indian Mounds Drive is valued for its environmental, recreational, transportation, and cultural benefits. These values represent community feedback throughout the study.', body, 1, '24-25');
}
function challenges(){
  var scale = '<div class="imd-chart"><div class="ct">Sense of safety</div><div class="imd-scale"><div class="ln"></div>' + [0,25,50,75,100].map(function(v){ return '<div class="tk" style="left:' + v + '%"></div>'; }).join('') + '<div class="mk" style="left:53.25%"><span>Avg safety rating</span></div><div class="lb" style="left:0">0 unsafe</div><div class="lb" style="left:100%">100 safe</div></div><div class="imd-axis"><span>Mean 53.25</span><span>Standard dev. 26.18</span></div></div>';
  var prior = '<div class="imd-chart"><div class="ct">Management priorities</div><div class="imd-prior">' + D.priorities.map(function(p){ return '<span>' + esc(p) + '</span>'; }).join('') + '</div></div>';
  var vmax = 90, vis = '<div class="imd-chart"><div class="ct">Annual visitor counts</div><svg class="col" viewBox="0 0 260 120" aria-label="Annual visitor counts 2020 to 2024">' +
    [10,20,30,40,50,60,70,80,90].map(function(k){ var y = 100 - k / vmax * 90; return '<line x1="34" x2="256" y1="' + y + '" y2="' + y + '" stroke="#e6e6e6"/><text x="30" y="' + (y + 3) + '" text-anchor="end" font-size="7" fill="#8a8f8f" font-family="Work Sans">' + k + 'k</text>'; }).join('') +
    D.visitors.map(function(v, i){ var h = v[1] / vmax * 90, x = 44 + i * 42; return '<rect x="' + x + '" y="' + (100 - h) + '" width="30" height="' + h + '" fill="#e05430"/><text x="' + (x + 15) + '" y="112" text-anchor="middle" font-size="7.5" fill="#545858" font-family="Work Sans">' + v[0] + '</text>'; }).join('') + '</svg><p class="imd-note">Read from the study’s chart to the nearest 5,000 (p. 27).</p></div>';
  var cards = [
    ['Safety','Several times while running on the drive I’ve seen cars driving the wrong direction, speeding, or driving too close to me which is scary.','Visitors are troubled by erratic driving and wrong-way traffic on the shared-use path, trash dumping, encampments, illicit activity, poor lighting, limited visibility, and few exit points.', scale],
    ['Operations & management','The jurisdictions change so much in the area, I wouldn’t know who to contact when witnessing dumping or wrong way drivers.','Maintaining the site is a constant and costly endeavor due to trash dumping, invasive species, erosion, flooding, and overgrown vegetation. Management responsibilities are shared among three municipalities, making it unclear who to contact for emergencies or reporting hazards.', prior],
    ['Site disturbance','The banks have been severely damaged and we need to take care of the ecological health of that stretch of riverbank.','Off-roading and mudding have deteriorated the river’s edge and adjacent lowland areas over time. Much of the area of disturbance has little to no vegetative cover and is subject to erosion and compaction, resulting in habitat loss and depletion of ecosystem function.', vis],
    ['Frequent flooding','It can be hard to predict when this connection is flooded or not.','Flooding is common and sometimes severe, complicating upkeep and causing washed-out trails, vehicle ruts, erosion, and debris blocking the road. Routes are sometimes impassable due to high water levels and there is not currently a strategy in place to notify users of obstacles ahead.', '<div class="imd-stat"><b>98<sup>%</sup></b><span>site area falls within the 100-year floodplain</span></div><a class="imd-btn" href="#imd-model" data-view="flood">' + icon('flood') + ' Flood the model to 605 feet</a>'],
    ['Illegal dumping','It is sad to see the way that people dump loads of trash along the River.','As a former landfill that hasn’t shaken its reputation, it is common to spot loads of trash, from heaps of yard waste to furniture, along Indian Mounds Drive. Cleanups take place throughout the year by concerned community members, volunteer organizations, and Indigenous communities. The municipalities of Grandville, Wyoming, and Grand Rapids perform routine maintenance. Maintenance work, especially when hazardous waste is involved, is very costly and difficult to keep up with.', '']
  ];
  var body = '<div class="imd-cards">' + cards.map(function(c){ return '<div class="imd-card"><div class="band"><h4>' + c[0] + '</h4></div><div class="bd"><q>' + esc(c[1]) + '</q><p>' + esc(c[2]) + '</p>' + c[3] + '</div></div>'; }).join('') + '</div>';
  return page('challenges', 'Community challenges', 'Indian Mounds Drive experiences a combination of safety concerns, environmental impacts, and maintenance challenges. These challenges, summarized from community feedback, affect both the ecological health and visitor experience in the corridor.', body, 1, '26-27');
}
function objectives(){
  var bubbles = [['b','It would be nice if there were better access points to the river. It’s difficult to navigate the trails from the road to the river.'],['o','Cohesive wayfinding & educational signage is important to create a more welcoming experience.'],['g','I know flooding may make it difficult, but could we make it more a destination than just a throughway?'],['b','Keep it a natural wooded area and limit vehicle access to assist with lowering the amount of trash dumping.'],['g','Resurfacing the road and painting bike and pedestrian lanes would be an amazing improvement!'],['o','I would like to see the banks of the river rebuilt with native plants and natural embankments.']];
  var left = '<div class="imd-bubbles">' + bubbles.map(function(b){ return '<span class="' + b[0] + '">“' + esc(b[1]) + '”</span>'; }).join('') + '</div>' +
    '<div class="imd-objectives"><img width="1125" height="293" src="' + ART + 'people.png" alt="Illustration of six people with arms around each other, from the study" loading="lazy"><h4>Community objectives</h4><ul><li>Resurfaced non-motorized trail</li><li>Nature trails & river access points</li><li>Wayfinding & interpretive signage</li><li>Basic comfort amenities</li><li>Green stormwater infrastructure & ecological restoration</li></ul></div>';
  var pr = [['ico-om.png','Unified operations & management strategy','A corridor that is consistently safe, clean, and free of trash, dumping, erosion scars, and lingering problem areas. Visible and reliable upkeep should be supported by routine monitoring and quick response to issues as they arise. A unified management approach signals that the space is actively cared for rather than neglected.'],
    ['ico-trail.png','Conversion to non-motorized trail','Close the road to public through-traffic to reduce dumping, ruts, erosion, and safety concerns resulting from pedestrian-vehicular conflicts, and create a non-motorized corridor designed primarily for walking, biking, and outdoor recreation. Preserve vehicular access primarily for maintenance and operations, emergency vehicles, and property owners. Over 70% of survey respondents supported the conversion.'],
    ['ico-culture.png','Cultural stewardship','Cultural assets in the project area will continue to be stewarded by local Tribes. Any improvements will be guided by the goals and vision of authorized Tribal representatives.'],
    ['ico-flood.png','Flood resiliency','Integrate thoughtful, resilient design solutions, including green stormwater infrastructure strategies, to improve watershed health and mitigate flood damage.'],
    ['ico-eco.png','Ecological restoration & recreational access','Concentrate improvements in areas that have already been disturbed, such as the road embankment or compaction zones. Visible ecological restoration efforts may include restored native habitat, riverbank protection, control of invasive species, and tree canopy management. Designating river access points and nature trails will minimize disturbance to the ecosystems and improve visitor experience.']];
  var right = '<h3 class="t" style="margin-top:4px">Guiding principles</h3><p class="lede">After input from thousands of community members and organizations, common themes emerged that revealed a shared vision for Indian Mounds Drive.</p><ul class="imd-principles">' + pr.map(function(p){ return '<li><img src="' + ART + p[0] + '" alt=""><div><h4>' + p[1] + '</h4><p>' + esc(p[2]) + '</p></div></li>'; }).join('') + '</ul>';
  var body = '<div class="imd-cols two"><div>' + left + '</div><div id="imd-principles">' + right + '</div></div>';
  return page('objectives', 'Community objectives', '', body, 2, '30-31');
}
function plan(){
  var body = '<div class="imd-cols two"><div>' + legend([['head','Proposed'],['line','#f06420','Trail reconstruction','See Trail Design Options, implemented along the main trail at the existing high ground, 12’ min. shared-use path in all other locations'],['line','#ec2878','Park entry drive','Two-way road to trailheads, terminating in a controlled access gate'],['dot','#fcdc18','Trailhead focus area','See focus area enlargements'],['pill','#287c54','River activation focus area','See focus area enlargements'],['head','Existing'],['dot','#acd474','Trailhead','Nearby parking and amenities'],['line','#5f8f7e','Recreational trail networks','A wealth of non-motorized trails connect to the corridor'],['blob','#d8e8ec','Open water','The Grand River, riparian wetlands, and a gravel pit lake'],['blob','#94c4b4','Wetland','Mixed hardwood swamps and marshes'],['line','#9aa6a6','Floodway','A zone where water actively flows during a 100-year flood'],['dash','#9aa6a6','Floodplain','The extents that water may rise during a 100-year flood']]) + '</div>' +
    '<div><p>Transforming the Indian Mounds Drive corridor into a recreational destination, this plan <b>closes the roadway to vehicles while maintaining controlled access for authorized users</b>, including public property owners (City of Grand Rapids, City of Grandville, City of Wyoming, Kent County Parks), private property owners (Goodale, Outdoor Systems, WYGR), authorized Tribal representatives, and GRPM stewards.</p><p>The plan establishes a <b>North Gateway trailhead at Market Ave.</b> and includes improvements to <b>Grandville Waterfront Park</b> and <b>Ivanrest Trailhead</b>, creating seamless access to the area. <b>Three river activation focus areas</b> are hubs for river connection and recreation, featuring nature trails and boardwalks, gathering spaces, interpretation, comfort amenities, and ecological restoration.</p>' +
    '<p>Focus areas on the plan, south to north: Waterfront Park South, Waterfront Park North, River Activation 1, Ivanrest Trailhead (on Kent Trails south of I-196), River Activation 2 (where Kent Trails meets the drive), River Activation 3 (at the Kent Trails Bridge), North Gateway at Market Ave.</p>' +
    '<a class="imd-btn solid" href="#imd-model" data-view="proposed">' + icon('cube') + ' See the overall plan in 3D</a></div></div>';
  return page('plan', 'Overall plan', '', body, 2, '32-33');
}
function trail(){
  var body = '<p>Reconstructing the main trail spine of Indian Mounds Drive can enhance the user experience through two possible trail design options. Implementation may include one or both options, informed by findings from the pilot project. Both concepts include supporting amenities such as <b>river-facing rest areas every 1/4 mile</b>, litter receptacles, wayfinding kiosks, interpretive signage, mile markers, <b>lighting at intersections and every 1/4 mile</b>, and <b>vehicle pull-offs every 1/8 mile</b>.</p>' +
    '<div class="imd-cols two"><div class="imd-section"><img width="1400" height="431" src="' + ART + 'section-base.webp" alt="Base trail cross section from the study" loading="lazy"><div class="cap"><b>Base trail</b>Follows the existing 22’ footprint of Indian Mounds Drive, with striping delineating a two-way bike facility and a pedestrian path. Bioswale 8’, pedestrian path 8’, two-way bike facility 7’ + 7’, bioswale 8’, on high ground 40’ to 66’ wide.</div></div>' +
    '<div class="imd-section"><img width="1400" height="431" src="' + ART + 'section-buffered.webp" alt="Buffered trail cross section from the study" loading="lazy"><div class="cap"><b>Buffered trail</b>Buffers the pedestrian path and the bike lane with a bioswale. Pedestrian path 8’, bioswale 8’, two-way bike facility 7’ + 7’. This wider footprint is dependent on funding, maintenance capabilities, permitting, and a detailed site survey with topography and tree locations.</div></div></div>' +
    '<p class="imd-note" style="margin-top:10px">Elevations on both sections: 588’ summer river elevation, 594’ ordinary high water mark (OHWM), 605’ 100-year flood elevation. The 3D model’s cross-section tool draws the same three lines on the real ground at any point along the drive. <a href="#imd-model" data-view="section">Open the cross section</a>.</p>';
  return page('trail', 'Trail design options', '', body, 2, '34-35');
}
function focus(){
  var body = '<div class="imd-focus">' + D.focus.map(function(f){
    return '<div class="imd-fa" id="imd-fa-' + f.id + '"><img src="' + ART + f.img + '" alt="Plan drawing of ' + esc(f.n) + ' from the study" loading="lazy"><div class="bd"><h4><i class="' + (f.kind === 'ra' ? 'ra' : '') + '" style="background:' + (f.kind === 'ra' ? '#287c54' : '#fcdc18') + '"></i>' + esc(f.n) + '</h4><p>' + esc(f.s) + '</p><ul>' + f.f.map(function(x){ return '<li>' + esc(x) + '</li>'; }).join('') + '</ul><div class="acts"><a class="imd-btn" href="#imd-model" data-fly="' + f.id + '">' + icon('cube') + ' Fly there in 3D</a><a class="imd-btn" href="' + pdfAt(f.page) + '" target="_blank" rel="noopener">' + icon('external') + ' PDF ' + pp(f.page, f.page + 1) + '</a></div><p class="imd-note" style="margin:8px 0 0">Scale ' + f.scale + '. Main trail layout to be selected from Trail Design Options; a 22’ trail is shown, following the existing road footprint.</p></div></div>';
  }).join('') + '</div>';
  return page('focus', 'Focus areas', 'Seven enlargements: four trailheads and three river activation areas. The drawings are the study’s; the 3D model places each one on the real corridor.', body, 2, '36-49');
}
function green(){
  var body = '<div class="imd-gi">' + D.gi.map(function(g){ return '<div><div class="band"><b>' + esc(g[0]) + '</b><span>' + esc(g[1]) + '</span></div><h6>Where to implement</h6><p style="font-size:12px">' + esc(g[2]) + '</p><h6>Benefits</h6><ul>' + g[3].map(function(b){ return '<li>' + esc(b) + '</li>'; }).join('') + '</ul></div>'; }).join('') + '</div>';
  return page('green', 'Green infrastructure guide', 'Nature-based infrastructure solutions are powerful tools to transform floodplain resiliency, biodiversity, and recreation quality. Opportunities abound throughout the project area, especially where development occurs, and are illustrated in the three River Activation focus areas.', body, 2, '50-51');
}
function om(){
  var body = '<div class="imd-cols two"><div><p>The transformation of Indian Mounds Drive into a non-motorized corridor presents an opportunity to improve a key link in the Grand River Greenway. The Grand River Greenway Operations & Management (O&M) Framework guides potential solutions for consistent, collaborative stewardship across the system’s 80+ miles.</p>' +
    '<h4 class="imd-h4">Greenway partners</h4><p>The core challenge of the Greenway is to build the practice of place management, going beyond basic maintenance toward a proactive, coordinated approach to the care, programming, and improvement of public spaces. Grand River Network was formed to serve as the backbone entity to connect partners and support cross-jurisdictional collaboration.</p>' +
    '<h4 class="imd-h4">Unified partnership agreement</h4>' + list(['Coordinate capital projects','Pursue funding mechanisms for implementation and maintenance','Adopt and pilot shared Operations & Maintenance Standards','Collaborate on a performance and accountability system with shared data collection and public reporting']) +
    '<h4 class="imd-h4">Operations & maintenance standards</h4>' + list(['Participate in the network-wide process of developing shared standards','Assess feasibility, costs, and performance of standards over time with a multi-jurisdictional leadership body']) + '</div>' +
    '<div><h4 class="imd-h4">Establish a sustainable funding system</h4>' + list(['Clarify a Greenway-specific budget line item in government partners’ annual budgets','Explore sustained Greenway-specific funding, such as property tax millages, Visitors Tax, etc.','Establish a stewardship fund for ongoing maintenance and enhancements from mixed public and private sources, investment earnings, and revenue avenues (sponsorships, user fees, etc.)']) +
    '<h4 class="imd-h4">Implementation plans</h4>' + list(['Develop a year-round activation plan with community events, public art, activities, and cultural programs','Advance a compelling Greenway identity with strategic branding','Strengthen public safety coordination with guidelines, safety protocols, and emergency response','Expand opportunities for local employment and small business participation','Integrate earned-revenue opportunities directly into activation and operations','Implement a clear and visible Greenway-wide wayfinding plan (West Michigan Trails Regional Wayfinding Guide)']) + '</div></div>';
  return page('om', 'Grand River Greenway O&M framework overview', '', body, 2, '52-53');
}
function unified(){
  var body = '<div class="imd-cols two"><div><h4 class="imd-h4">Proposed management structure</h4><p>Municipal ownership of the corridor would be retained, and operations and maintenance responsibilities may be transferred to Kent County Parks through a lease agreement, easement, Urban Cooperation Act Agreement, or similar partnership mechanism.</p><p>Prior to trail conversion, project partners will clarify Act 51 implications, determining whether formal road abandonment is required, and resolve remaining land control and governance considerations.</p><p><b>Ownership and management of the Grand River Burial Mounds site is unchanged, and will continue to be stewarded by local tribes and the Grand Rapids Public Museum.</b></p>' +
    '<h4 class="imd-h4">Operations and maintenance</h4><p>Kent County Parks would serve as the primary operator of the trail and recreation area:</p>' + list(['Routine maintenance','Safety inspections','Vegetation management','Signage maintenance','Trail amenity upkeep','Capital repairs and replacements']) + '<p>Municipal partners could provide targeted services where efficiencies exist, such as snow removal.</p></div>' +
    '<div><h4 class="imd-h4">Partnership agreement</h4><p>Agencies will establish an Urban Cooperation Act Agreement outlining governance structure, operations responsibilities, maintenance responsibilities, and cost-sharing methodology. The agreement could be modeled after the existing Kent Trails partnership. Costs would be invoiced annually based on a predetermined cost-sharing formula.</p>' +
    '<h4 class="imd-h4">Next steps</h4><ul class="plain imd-steps"><li><b>Q4 2026:</b> Develop and refine partnership details and agreement terms.</li><li><b>Q1 2027:</b> Present the proposed agreement to partner legislative bodies for consideration.</li><li><b>Q2 2027:</b> Implement the pilot program and operational framework, pending consideration.</li></ul>' +
    '<h4 class="imd-h4">Land ownership</h4>' + legend(D.ownership.map(function(o){ return ['blob', o[0], o[1], '']; })) + '</div></div>';
  return page('unified', 'Unified operations & management strategy', '', body, 2, '54-55');
}
function phasing(){
  var body = '<div class="imd-cols two"><div><div class="imd-phasehead">Pilot project: 2027-2029</div><img width="751" height="634" src="' + ART + 'map-pilot.webp" alt="Pilot project map: access control at both ends and signage along the drive" style="width:100%;max-width:420px;display:block;border:1px solid var(--i-rule)" loading="lazy">' +
    '<p style="margin-top:10px">To address urgent concerns of user safety and degradation of the corridor, municipal partners will temporarily implement a new management model, measure its performance, and use the results to guide future investment and management decisions during a <b>two-year test period, beginning in the 2nd quarter of 2027</b>.</p>' +
    '<p class="imd-h5">Preparation</p>' + list(['Advanced public communication about upcoming changes','Execute the partnership agreement, outlining responsibilities and a cost sharing program','Develop evaluation criteria for measuring the project’s success','Implement two controlled vehicular access gates ($70,000 to $80,000 total)','Complete surfacing spot repairs as necessary ($0 to $350,000)','Install basic trail wayfinding, mile markers, and temporary project signs ($2,500)','Install basic site amenities ($5,000)']) +
    '<p class="imd-h5">During project duration</p>' + list(['Pilot the Unified Operations & Management Strategy','Visibly elevate public safety presence, with particular attention to illegal vehicle activity, dumping, vandalism, and other prohibited activity','Meet regularly with the stakeholder group to evaluate the project and plan for future phases, including fundraising and grant applications']) +
    '<p class="imd-cost">Total upfront cost: $77,500 to $437,500 · Estimated annual operations and maintenance cost: $15,000</p>' + legend([['line','#e05430','Access control',''],['dot','#fcdc18','Signage','']]) + '<a class="imd-btn" href="#imd-model" data-view="pilot" style="margin-top:10px">' + icon('cube') + ' Show the pilot in 3D</a></div>' +
    '<div><div class="imd-phasehead">Future project modules</div><img width="755" height="634" src="' + ART + 'map-future.webp" alt="Future project modules map" style="width:100%;max-width:420px;display:block;border:1px solid var(--i-rule)" loading="lazy"><p style="margin-top:10px">The future vision can be accomplished in project modules over the long term. During the pilot phase, partners will collaborate to prepare for future stages. As funding is secured, the modules can be pursued; the most cost-efficient delivery constructs them simultaneously when possible.</p>' +
    '<div class="imd-module"><i class="line"></i><div><h5>Trail reconstruction</h5><p>For highest cost-efficiency and to reduce potential damage to newly constructed projects, strongly consider constructing River Activation areas and the North Gateway at the same time as the trail, if funding allows. A trail design will need to be selected from the two options.</p></div></div>' +
    '<div class="imd-module"><i class="dot"></i><div><h5>North Gateway at Market Ave.</h5><p>New trailhead on the north end of the site provides critical access from downtown Grand Rapids and neighboring communities.</p></div></div>' +
    '<div class="imd-module"><i class="pill"></i><div><h5>River activation areas</h5><p>Improves river access and recreation along the main trail.</p></div></div>' +
    '<div class="imd-module"><i class="dot"></i><div><h5>Ivanrest Trailhead</h5><p>Improves existing trailhead with comfort amenities, expanded programming, and activation potential along Kent Trails.</p></div></div>' +
    '<div class="imd-module"><i class="dot"></i><div><h5>Waterfront Park</h5><p>Improves existing trailhead function, connectivity, and circulation at the southwest end of Indian Mounds Drive.</p></div></div>' +
    '<a class="imd-btn" href="#imd-model" data-view="future">' + icon('cube') + ' Show the modules in 3D</a></div></div>';
  return page('phasing', 'Phasing plan', 'This phasing plan begins with a pilot project to address immediate safety concerns and verify the long-term vision as a dedicated recreational trail.', body, 2, '56-57');
}
function funding(){
  var body = '<p>Implementation will likely be achieved through a combination of municipal and county capital funding, state and federal grants, Tribal partnerships, philanthropic contributions, private donations, and corporate or community sponsorships. Leveraging multiple funding sources will allow improvements to be phased over time.</p><div class="imd-cols two">' +
    D.grants.map(function(g){ return '<div class="imd-kv"><span class="imd-bandh">' + esc(g.t) + '</span><p style="font-size:13px">' + esc(g.d) + '</p><b>Eligibility</b><ul>' + g.e.map(function(x){ return '<li>' + esc(x) + '</li>'; }).join('') + '</ul>' + (g.r ? '<b>Permits & review</b><ul>' + g.r.map(function(x){ return '<li>' + esc(x) + '</li>'; }).join('') + '</ul>' : '') + '<b>Deadlines</b><ul><li>' + esc(g.dl) + '</li></ul><b>Grant amount</b><p class="amt">' + esc(g.amt) + '</p></div>'; }).join('') +
    '<div class="imd-kv"><span class="imd-bandh">Auxiliary grants</span>' + D.aux.map(function(a){ return '<b style="color:var(--i-green-txt)">' + esc(a[0]) + '</b><p style="font-size:12.5px;margin:2px 0 0">' + esc(a[1]) + '</p><p class="amt">' + esc(a[2]) + '</p>'; }).join('') + '<p style="font-size:12.5px;margin-top:10px">Most auxiliary grants listed require a 20% match; the Five Star and Urban Waters Restoration Program requires a 25% match. A higher local match percentage generally positions an application to be more competitive.</p></div></div>';
  return page('funding', 'Grants & funding sources', '', body, 2, '58-59');
}
function permits(){
  var body = '<p>Implementation of the recommendations will require coordination with a variety of regulatory agencies and permitting authorities. Early coordination with permitting agencies will help identify potential constraints, streamline the review process, and reduce project delays.</p><div class="imd-cols two">' +
    '<div class="imd-kv"><span class="imd-bandh blue">Environment, Great Lakes, and Energy (EGLE)</span><p class="imd-note" style="font-style:italic">90 day review period + 90 days public hearing, if required</p><p style="font-size:13px">EGLE/USACE Joint Permit Applications (JPA) regulate construction activities in the 100-year floodplain and floodway. The application will evaluate if the activities will create a net neutral or positive impact.</p><b>Considerations</b><ul><li>Locate development in areas of the site that are already disturbed</li><li>Schedule a pre-application meeting to avoid delays</li><li>Any potential negative impacts to waterways will require ecological enhancements to ensure a net neutral or positive impact</li></ul>' +
    '<span class="imd-bandh blue">Section 106: National Historic Preservation Act of 1966</span><p style="font-size:13px">Required when a project uses federal funds or requires a federal license or permit, this process ensures the protection of historic and cultural properties.</p><b>Special considerations</b><ul><li>Appropriate consulting parties include the federal agency, the State Historic Preservation Officer, the Tribal Historic Preservation Officer, and the Advisory Council on Historic Preservation</li></ul></div>' +
    '<div class="imd-kv"><span class="imd-bandh blue">Local agency construction within MDOT trunkline right of way</span><p style="font-size:13px">MDOT requires that the Local Agency (LA) obtain an individual MDOT construction permit for all work within the trunkline right of way. The project area includes a right of way for both I-196 and M-11 (Wilson Ave) trunklines.</p><b>Special considerations</b><ul><li>The LA must obtain all permits and approvals related to its construction project and forward them to the MDOT Local Agency Program (LAP) staff engineer before LAP can request obligation of federal or state funds</li><li>Contact the permit engineer of the appropriate MDOT Transportation Service Center (TSC) early in the design phase</li></ul></div></div>';
  return page('permits', 'Permitting requirements', '', body, 2, '60-61');
}
function sources(){
  var body = '<div class="imd-sources"><ul class="plain"><li><a href="' + PDF + '" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Indian Mounds Drive Feasibility Study (PDF, 31 spreads, about 62 MB)</a>, Kent County Parks and Grand River Network, prepared by VIRIDIS Design Group and Fishbeck, October 2026. Page numbers above are the printed ones.</li>' +
    '<li><a href="' + PAGE + '" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kent County: Indian Mounds Drive Study</a>, the project page with the survey summary, open house notes, partners, timeline and funding.</li>' +
    '<li><a href="' + P1 + '" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Phase 1 Community Engagement Findings (PDF)</a> and <a href="' + P2 + '" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Phase 2 April Open House Findings (PDF)</a>.</li>' +
    '<li>3D model geography: OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL) for the river, roads, trails, parks and city limits; FEMA National Flood Hazard Layer for the floodway and 100-year floodplain; USGS 3DEP for the ground. Proposed features are placed by hand from the study’s plan drawings and scale bars, so treat their positions as illustrative, within a few tens of feet.</li></ul>' +
    '<h4 class="imd-h4">Left out on purpose</h4><ul class="plain"><li>The Grand River Burial Mounds: no location, outline, form or label, in the text or the model. The study names the site, its National Historic Landmark status, the 1982 fence and the tribe-led rule, and that is all that is repeated here.</li><li>The study’s photographs, except the plan drawings, sections, maps and icons shown as small figures with their page cited.</li><li>Land ownership parcels and “areas of disturbance”, which exist only as drawings in the PDF, are described but not modelled.</li><li>The appendix (Opinion of Probable Costs, outreach documentation) is not in the public PDF.</li></ul></div>';
  return page('sources', 'Sources and what is left out', '', body, 0, '1-61');
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------ render: the map first, then visual cards, the text behind More */
var SHEETS = {};
function sheetFor(id, title, html){ SHEETS[id] = {t:title, h:html}; return id; }
function card(opts){
  /* one graphic or big number, one line of at most about 15 words, and a More button */
  return '<div class="imd-c' + (opts.wide ? ' wide' : '') + '" id="imd-c-' + opts.id + '">' +
    (opts.big ? '<div class="big' + (opts.cls ? ' ' + opts.cls : '') + '">' + opts.big + '</div>' : '') +
    (opts.vis || '') +
    '<p>' + opts.line + '</p>' +
    '<div class="row">' + (opts.act || '') + '<button class="imd-more" data-sheet="' + opts.sheet + '">More ' + icon('chevron') + '</button><span class="imd-cite">' + (opts.cite || '') + '</span></div></div>';
}
function miniBars(rows, n){
  var max = rows[0][1];
  return '<div class="imd-mini">' + rows.slice(0, n).map(function(r){ return '<span>' + esc(r[0]) + '</span><i style="width:' + (r[1] / max * 100) + '%;background:' + r[2] + '"></i><b>' + r[1] + '%</b>'; }).join('') + '</div>';
}
function render(){
  var root = document.getElementById('imd-root');
  if(!root) return;
  /* the full detail, by sheet */
  sheetFor('corridor', 'The corridor', intro() + context() + inventory() + ownership() + recreation() + regional());
  sheetFor('voices', 'What people said', input() + values());
  sheetFor('challenges', 'The challenges', challenges());
  sheetFor('traffic', 'Traffic study', traffic());
  sheetFor('plan', 'The plan', objectives() + plan() + trail());
  sheetFor('focus', 'Seven focus areas', focus());
  sheetFor('phases', 'Phasing, management, money', phasing() + unified() + om() + funding() + permits());
  sheetFor('history', 'Site history', history());
  sheetFor('green', 'Green infrastructure guide', green());
  sheetFor('sacred', 'A sacred site', sacred() + '<p class="imd-note">From the study, pp. 2, 4, 24, 31 and 54, and the Kent County study page.</p>');
  sheetFor('who', 'Who is involved', contents());
  sheetFor('sources', 'Sources and what is left out', sources());

  var thumbs = '<div class="imd-thumbs">' + D.focus.map(function(f){ return '<button data-fly="' + f.id + '" title="' + esc(f.n) + '"><img src="' + ART + f.img + '" alt="" loading="lazy" width="1400" height="692"><span><i style="background:' + (f.kind === 'ra' ? '#287c54' : '#fcdc18') + '"></i>' + esc(f.n.replace(' Trailhead', '').replace(' at Market Ave.', '')) + '</span></button>'; }).join('') + '</div>';
  var phases = '<div class="imd-strip"><div class="on"><b>2027-29</b>Pilot: two gates, signs, repairs<small>$77,500 to $437,500</small></div><div><b>Then</b>Trail rebuilt, 22’ wide<small>Base or buffered</small></div><div><b>As funded</b>Trailheads, river areas<small>Built together where possible</small></div></div>';
  var years = '<div class="imd-years">' + D.history.map(function(e){ return '<span title="' + esc(e.t) + '"><b>' + esc(e.yr) + '</b>' + esc(e.t) + '</span>'; }).join('') + '</div>';
  var who = '<div class="imd-who"><b>Kent County Parks</b><b>Grand River Network</b><b>Downtown Grand Rapids Inc.</b><b>Grand Rapids</b><b>Grandville</b><b>Wyoming</b><b>Grand Rapids Public Museum</b><b>Grand Valley Metro Council</b><b>Kent County Road Commission</b><b>MDOT</b><b>Grand River Bands of Ottawa Indians</b><b>Gun Lake Tribe</b><b>VIRIDIS Design Group</b><b>Fishbeck</b></div>';
  var gi = '<div class="imd-who sm">' + D.gi.map(function(g){ return '<b>' + esc(g[0]) + '</b>'; }).join('') + '</div>';

  root.innerHTML =
    '<p class="imd-strap">Indian Mounds Drive Feasibility Study · Kent County Parks and Grand River Network · October 2026 · <a href="' + PDF + '" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the PDF</a></p>' +
    '<div class="imd-model" id="imd-model"><div class="imd-topbar" id="imd-bar"></div><div class="imd-stage" id="imd-stage"><div class="loading">Loading the corridor…</div></div><div class="imd-flood" id="imd-flood"></div><div class="imd-xpanel" id="imd-xpanel" hidden></div></div>' +
    '<div class="imd-grid">' +
    card({id:'corridor', sheet:'corridor', big:'4 miles', line:'Wilson Ave. to Market Ave., between the Grand River and I-196, across from Millennium Park.', vis:'<img class="vis" src="' + ART + 'map-inventory.webp" alt="Site inventory map" loading="lazy" width="1200" height="925">', cite:'pp. 6-13', act:'<a class="imd-btn" href="#imd-model" data-view="inventory">' + icon('cube') + ' Existing</a>'}) +
    card({id:'voices', sheet:'voices', big:'1,984 voices', line:'Online survey, 107 at the open house, over 2,100 people in all. Cycling leads.', vis:miniBars(D.uses, 4), cite:'pp. 22-23'}) +
    card({id:'safety', sheet:'plan', big:'Safety first', line:'Close the road to through-traffic. A 22-foot trail: 8’ path and a two-way bike lane.', vis:'<img class="vis" src="' + ART + 'section-base.webp" alt="Base trail cross section" loading="lazy" width="1400" height="431">', cite:'pp. 31-35', act:'<a class="imd-btn" href="#imd-model" data-view="section">' + icon('ruler') + ' Section</a>', wide:true}) +
    card({id:'wild', sheet:'voices', big:'87<sup>%</sup>', line:'want it to stay natural, quiet and wild. Over 70% back a car-free trail.', cite:'pp. 24-25'}) +
    card({id:'worries', sheet:'challenges', big:'53 of 100', line:'Average sense of safety. Wrong-way cars, dumping and floods top the list; 98% of the site floods.', vis:'<div class="imd-scale sm"><div class="ln"></div><div class="mk" style="left:53.25%"></div><div class="lb" style="left:0">Unsafe</div><div class="lb" style="left:100%">Safe</div></div>', cite:'pp. 26-27', act:'<a class="imd-btn" href="#imd-model" data-view="flood">' + icon('flood') + ' 605 ft</a>'}) +
    card({id:'traffic', sheet:'traffic', big:'60<sup>%</sup> on foot or bike', line:'Seven days, five count sites, June 2026. 8% of cars drove the wrong way.', vis:donut(D.traffic.split), cite:'pp. 16-17', act:'<a class="imd-btn" href="#imd-model" data-view="traffic">' + icon('people') + ' Sites</a>'}) +
    card({id:'green', sheet:'green', vis:'<img class="ico" src="' + ART + 'ico-eco.png" alt="">' + gi, big:'Six green tools', line:'Bioswales, wetland and stream restoration, bank stabilization, filter strips, naturalized culverts.', cite:'pp. 50-51'}) +
    card({id:'focus', sheet:'focus', big:'7 focus areas', line:'Four trailheads and three river activation areas. Tap one to fly there.', vis:thumbs, cite:'pp. 36-49', wide:true}) +
    card({id:'phases', sheet:'phases', big:'3 steps', line:'A two-year pilot from spring 2027, then the trail, then the trailheads and river areas as money comes.', vis:phases, cite:'pp. 54-61', act:'<a class="imd-btn" href="#imd-model" data-view="pilot">' + icon('cube') + ' Pilot</a>', wide:true}) +
    card({id:'history', sheet:'history', big:'2,000 years', line:'Burial mounds, a treaty, a railroad, a WPA road, a freeway, a landfill, a park.', vis:years, cite:'pp. 10-11', wide:true}) +
    card({id:'sacred', sheet:'sacred', vis:'<img class="ico" src="' + ART + 'ico-culture.png" alt="">', big:'Sacred ground', line:'The burial mounds are sacred. Decisions about them are tribe-led. Nothing here locates them.', cite:'pp. 4, 24, 54', cls:'green'}) +
    card({id:'sources', sheet:'sources', big:'Sources', line:'The study PDF, the county page, OpenStreetMap, USGS and FEMA. What was left out, and why.', cite:'', act:'<a class="imd-btn" href="' + PDF + '" target="_blank" rel="noopener">' + icon('external') + ' PDF</a>'}) +
    card({id:'who', sheet:'who', big:'Who’s involved', line:'Led by Kent County Parks, funded by DGRI through a 2022 DNR grant, designed by VIRIDIS and Fishbeck.', vis:who, cite:'p. 2', wide:true}) +
    '</div>';

  root.addEventListener('click', function(e){
    var more = e.target.closest('[data-sheet]');
    if(more){ openSheet(more.dataset.sheet); return; }
    var fly = e.target.closest('button[data-fly]');
    if(fly){ document.getElementById('imd-model').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth', block:'start'}); if(window.IMD_MODEL) window.IMD_MODEL.fly(fly.dataset.fly); return; }
    var a = e.target.closest('a[href^="#imd-"]');
    if(!a) return;
    var id = a.getAttribute('href').slice(1), el = document.getElementById(id);
    e.preventDefault();
    var M = window.IMD_MODEL;
    if(el) el.scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth', block:'start'});
    if(M){ if(a.dataset.fly) M.fly(a.dataset.fly); else if(a.dataset.view) M.preset(a.dataset.view); }
  });
  if(window.IMD_MODEL && window.IMD_MODEL.init) window.IMD_MODEL.init();
}
/* the sheet: full detail, page numbers and the PDF link, over the page */
var sheetEl = null;
function openSheet(id){
  var sh = SHEETS[id]; if(!sh) return;
  closeSheet();
  sheetEl = document.createElement('div'); sheetEl.className = 'imd-sheet'; sheetEl.id = 'imd';   /* so #imd- anchors inside still scope to this tab's styles */
  sheetEl.id = '';
  sheetEl.innerHTML = '<div class="imd-sheet-in" id="imd-sheet"><div class="hd"><span>' + esc(sh.t) + '</span><button class="x" aria-label="Close">× Close</button></div><div class="bd">' + sh.h + '</div></div>';
  document.body.appendChild(sheetEl); document.body.style.overflow = 'hidden';
  sheetEl.querySelector('.x').addEventListener('click', closeSheet);
  sheetEl.addEventListener('click', function(e){
    if(e.target === sheetEl){ closeSheet(); return; }
    var a = e.target.closest('a[href^="#imd-"]');
    if(!a) return;
    e.preventDefault();
    var id2 = a.getAttribute('href').slice(1), inSheet = sheetEl.querySelector('#' + id2);
    if(inSheet && !a.dataset.view && !a.dataset.fly){ inSheet.scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth', block:'start'}); return; }
    closeSheet();
    var M = window.IMD_MODEL, model = document.getElementById('imd-model');
    if(model) model.scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth', block:'start'});
    if(M){ if(a.dataset.fly) M.fly(a.dataset.fly); else if(a.dataset.view) M.preset(a.dataset.view); }
  });
  document.addEventListener('keydown', escClose);
}
function escClose(e){ if(e.key === 'Escape') closeSheet(); }
function closeSheet(){ if(sheetEl){ sheetEl.remove(); sheetEl = null; document.body.style.overflow = ''; document.removeEventListener('keydown', escClose); } }

/* ------------------------------------------------------------ knowledge for Ask and Facts */
var KBX = [
  {id:'imd-study', t:'Indian Mounds Drive feasibility study', f:['build','people'], reach:'CORRIDOR', c:'official',
   tags:'indian mounds drive feasibility study kent county parks grand river network viridis fishbeck greenway grandville wyoming survey open house 2026 dgri dnr grant phase plan corridor trail',
   html:'<p>Kent County Parks and Grand River Network published the Indian Mounds Drive Feasibility Study in October 2026, prepared by VIRIDIS Design Group and Fishbeck and funded by Downtown Grand Rapids Inc. through a 2022 Michigan DNR grant to grow the Grand River Greenway. The study covers four miles of Indian Mounds Drive and 363 acres between the Grand River and I-196, from Wilson Avenue in Grandville to Market Avenue in Grand Rapids, across the river from Millennium Park (pp. 4-9).</p><dl class="kv"><dt>Process</dt><dd>Phase 1, August to December 2025: online survey and focus groups. Phase 2, January to May 2026: design scenarios, open house, focus groups. Phase 3, summer 2026: preferred plan and report; plan reveal open house (pp. 4, 22).</dd><dt>Participation</dt><dd>1,984 public online survey responses, 107 people at Open House #1 (April 27, 2026), over 2,100 participants in all. 98% of respondents were aware of the project area (p. 22).</dd><dt>Steering team</dt><dd>Grand Rapids, Grandville, Wyoming, Downtown Grand Rapids Inc., Grand Rapids Public Museum, Grand Valley Metro Council, Kent County Parks, Kent County Road Commission, MDOT; Grand River Bands of Ottawa Indians and Gun Lake Tribe were consulted throughout and asked that the results of their consultation not be shared broadly (p. 2).</dd><dt>Source</dt><dd><a href="' + PDF + '" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The study PDF</a> and the <a href="' + PAGE + '" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kent County study page</a>. River Brain has a full deep dive under the Mounds Dr tab.</dd></dl>'},
  {id:'imd-site', t:'Indian Mounds Drive, the corridor today', f:['history','water'], reach:'CORRIDOR', c:'stable',
   tags:'indian mounds drive corridor one-way road wrong way floodplain flooding i-196 millennium park kent trails wilson market grandville wyoming history wpa 1936 railroad 1869 landfill dumping grand lady gravel oil wells',
   html:'<p>A 4-mile former two-way road, now one-way northbound for vehicles with a shared-use trail on the other lane, on high ground built by a WPA project in 1936 to control flooding. Cars can only legally enter from Wilson Avenue and exit onto Market Avenue; the study counted an average of 8% wrong-way vehicles over seven days. About 98% of the site area falls within the 100-year floodplain and flooding is frequent, with washed-out trails, ruts and debris (pp. 8, 12, 16, 27).</p><p>Timeline from the study (pp. 10-11): burial mounds built from about 100 AD; 1821 Treaty of Chicago; 1869 Lake Shore and Michigan Southern Railroad swing bridge (now the Kent Trails Bridge to Millennium Park); 1904 Grand Rapids Parks and Boulevard Association scenic drive, deeded to the city in 1921; 1936 WPA road; 1939 oil drilling, later gravel mining to 1976; 1962 Interstate 196, shifted south after Indigenous activists objected to a route through the mounds; 1970 Grandville landfill closes, dumping persists, a fence around the burial mounds property in 1982; 1993 Grand Lady riverboat, moved downstream in 2011 for flooding; 1995 Kent Trails; 2004 Millennium Park.</p><p>Landowners along the road: the cities of Grandville, Grand Rapids and Wyoming own the river-edge parcels; Kent County, Goodale Enterprises, Outdoor Systems and WYGR LLC hold adjacent land; MDOT holds the trunkline right of way. It is unclear from the title search whether a defined right-of-way exists for the roadway (p. 14). The county page adds about 200 vehicles a day, a radio tower, a freeway billboard and infrequently used oil wells on private land.</p>'},
  {id:'imd-community', t:'What the community said about Indian Mounds Drive', f:['people'], reach:'CORRIDOR', c:'official',
   tags:'indian mounds drive survey community values challenges safety dumping wilderness conversion non-motorized 70 percent 87 percent cycling walking fishing events marathon river bank run millennium park kent trails',
   html:'<p>Values (pp. 24-25): 87% want the area to keep its natural, quiet, wilderness feel; over 70% support converting Indian Mounds Drive to a non-motorized greenway trail. Top uses: cycling 57%, walking or running 45%, driving through 31%, wildlife viewing 23%, events 21%, fishing 8%, cultural or spiritual 2%. Visitation: 41% a few times a year, 22% monthly, 19% weekly or more, 12% once a year, 6% never. Most important connections: Millennium Park and Kent Trails first, then downtown Grand Rapids and Grandville neighbourhoods (p. 22-24).</p><p>Challenges (pp. 26-27): wrong-way and erratic driving, dumping, encampments, poor lighting and few exits; a mean sense-of-safety rating of 53.25 out of 100 (standard deviation 26.18); management split among three municipalities so nobody knows whom to call; off-roading that stripped the riverbank; frequent flooding; a former landfill that still attracts dumping. Management priorities in order: consistent coordinated management, basic cleanliness and repair, sustainable funding, public safety and enforcement, flood-resilient maintenance, ecological restoration.</p><p>The 2025 survey summary on the county page names five themes: safety first, maintenance needs attention, ecological restoration as a top priority, respect for sacred sites (decisions about the mounds must be tribe-led), and a calm, natural experience.</p>'},
  {id:'imd-traffic', t:'Indian Mounds Drive traffic study, June 2026', f:['people','build'], reach:'CORRIDOR', c:'official',
   tags:'indian mounds drive traffic study counts cyclists pedestrians motorists wrong way site kent trails bridge market avenue waterfront park ivanrest',
   html:'<p>Over seven days, June 16 to 22, 2026, five sites counted driving, cycling and walking or running. Non-motorized users were 60% of all movements (cyclists 45%, pedestrians 15%) against 40% motorists. Site #1 Grandville Waterfront Park: 461 pedestrians, 1,322 cyclists. Site #2 begin one-way road: 252 pedestrians, 1,266 cyclists, 1,526 motorists. Site #3 Kent Trails to Ivanrest: 108 pedestrians, 723 cyclists. Site #4 Kent Trails Bridge to Millennium Park: 790 pedestrians, 1,933 cyclists. Site #5 Market Avenue connection: 23 pedestrians, 265 cyclists, 1,400 motorists. An average of 8% of vehicles drove the wrong way, nearly twice as many at the south end. Activity peaked at lunchtime and evening for cars, mid-day for cycling, and around 8am and 7pm for walking and running; Friday to Sunday were busiest (pp. 16-17).</p>'},
  {id:'imd-plan', t:'The preferred plan for Indian Mounds Drive', f:['build'], reach:'CORRIDOR', c:'official',
   tags:'indian mounds drive plan recommendations close road non-motorized trail controlled access gate north gateway market avenue waterfront park ivanrest trailhead river activation boardwalk nature trail kayak launch boat launch parking trail design base buffered bioswale 605 594 588 flood elevation guiding principles',
   html:'<p>The plan closes the roadway to public through-traffic while keeping controlled access for authorized users (the three cities, Kent County Parks, the private owners Goodale, Outdoor Systems and WYGR, authorized Tribal representatives and Grand Rapids Public Museum stewards). It adds a North Gateway trailhead at Market Avenue, improves Grandville Waterfront Park (south and north trailheads) and Ivanrest Trailhead, and creates three River Activation focus areas with nature trails, boardwalks over wetland restoration, gathering spaces, interpretation, comfort amenities and ecological restoration (pp. 32-49).</p><p>Guiding principles (p. 31): unified operations and management; conversion to non-motorized trail; cultural stewardship (cultural assets continue to be stewarded by local Tribes, improvements guided by authorized Tribal representatives); flood resiliency; ecological restoration and recreational access concentrated in already disturbed areas. Community objectives (p. 30): resurfaced non-motorized trail, nature trails and river access points, wayfinding and interpretive signage, basic comfort amenities, green stormwater infrastructure and ecological restoration.</p><p>Trail design options (pp. 34-35): the Base Trail keeps the existing 22-foot footprint with an 8-foot pedestrian path and a 7+7-foot two-way bike facility between 8-foot bioswales; the Buffered Trail puts an 8-foot bioswale between the 8-foot pedestrian path and the 7+7-foot bike facility, a wider footprint dependent on funding, maintenance, permitting and survey. Both on high ground 40 to 66 feet wide, with river-facing rest areas every quarter mile, lighting at intersections and every quarter mile, pull-offs every eighth mile, litter receptacles, kiosks, interpretive signs and mile markers. Section elevations: 588 feet summer river, 594 feet ordinary high water mark, 605 feet 100-year flood.</p><p>Focus area details: Waterfront Park South, 58 parking spots plus 10 trailer spots, boat launch and skid pier, river access steps, picnic area in the poplar grove, 12-foot shared-use trail (p. 36). Waterfront Park North, universally accessible kayak launch, plaza with picnic tables and kiosk, vehicular access control gate, 7, 7 and 7+4 parking spots, 12-foot and 10-foot shared-use paths, and a wider protected crossing on the Wilson Avenue Bridge (p. 38). Ivanrest, 28 parking spots, restroom, picnic pavilion, lawn, Roys Creek overlook, floodplain restoration (p. 40). North Gateway, 23 parking spots, gate, kiosk and bike repair station, retaining wall with guardrail (p. 42). River Activation 1, 2 and 3: overlooks, elevated boardwalks over wetland restoration, barrier-free nature trails, river access points, rest areas, picnic groves, a 5-spot pull-off at the Kent Trails Bridge (pp. 44-49).</p>'},
  {id:'imd-implementation', t:'Indian Mounds Drive: phasing, management, funding, permits', f:['build','people'], reach:'CORRIDOR', c:'planned',
   tags:'indian mounds drive pilot 2027 2029 gates cost phasing kent county parks operator urban cooperation act agreement act 51 grants orlp lwcf trust fund tap build permits egle section 106 mdot',
   html:'<p>Pilot project, 2027 to 2029 (pp. 56-57): a two-year test beginning in the second quarter of 2027 with two controlled vehicular access gates ($70,000 to $80,000), surfacing spot repairs ($0 to $350,000), basic wayfinding, mile markers and temporary signs ($2,500) and basic amenities ($5,000). Total upfront cost $77,500 to $437,500, estimated annual operations and maintenance $15,000. Future modules as funding allows: trail reconstruction, North Gateway at Market Avenue, river activation areas, Ivanrest Trailhead, Waterfront Park, ideally built together.</p><p>Management (pp. 54-55): municipalities keep ownership; operations and maintenance may transfer to Kent County Parks by lease, easement or an Urban Cooperation Act Agreement modelled on the Kent Trails partnership, with costs invoiced annually by formula. Partners will clarify Act 51 implications (whether formal road abandonment is required). Next steps: Q4 2026 draft the agreement, Q1 2027 present it to partner legislative bodies, Q2 2027 start the pilot. Ownership and management of the Grand River Burial Mounds site is unchanged, stewarded by local tribes and the Grand Rapids Public Museum.</p><p>Funding (pp. 58-59): ORLP ($300,000 to $15 million, deadlines April 1 and September 1), Land and Water Conservation Fund ($30,000 to $500,000, April 1), Natural Resources Trust Fund ($15,000 to $400,000, April 1), plus BUILD ($1 million to $25 million), Five Star and Urban Waters ($30,000 to $150,000, 25% match), Michigan TAP (minimum $200,000) and TA Set-Aside ($50,000 to $2 million); most need a 20% match. Permits (pp. 60-61): EGLE/USACE Joint Permit Application for work in the floodplain and floodway (90-day review plus 90 days of public hearing if required, net neutral or positive impact), Section 106 review when federal funds are involved, and MDOT permits for work in the I-196 and M-11 right of way.</p>'},
  {id:'imd-sacred', t:'The Grand River Burial Mounds and the study', f:['history','people'], reach:'CORRIDOR', c:'stable',
   tags:'burial mounds sacred norton indigenous anishinaabe anishinabek grand river bands ottawa gun lake tribe national historic landmark public museum tribe-led interpretation access visibility',
   html:'<p>The study calls the Grand River Burial Mounds site a National Historic Landmark within the study area and an ancient cemetery for Indigenous ancestors, a sacred and protected space visited by Anishinabek for meditation, prayer and spiritual practices. Any decisions on the Mounds site and adjacent Indian Mounds Drive are guided by Native voices and Tribal consultation (pp. 4, 24). Grand River Bands of Ottawa Indians and Gun Lake Tribe were consulted throughout and asked that the results not be shared broadly (p. 2). Ownership and management of the site is unchanged and stays with local tribes and the Grand Rapids Public Museum (p. 54). The county page counts 11 mounds about 2,000 years old, the only surviving mound group in the lower Grand River Valley, and says decisions about visibility, access, interpretation or educational materials must be tribe-led.</p><p><strong>River Brain rule:</strong> do not locate, describe access to, or speculate about the mounds beyond these public statements. Point people to the Grand River Bands of Ottawa Indians and the Grand Rapids Public Museum.</p>'}
];
